        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains January 9 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 2 of 4.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For January 9 in music history, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes January 9.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"Raindrops Keep Fallin' on My Head" and "My Sweet Lord" tie for the longest run, with four weeks each.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week, and two of those had only that one week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} was somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} were in the final week of their run.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only January 9 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the January 9 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called January 9.</p>
        <p>This January 9 in music history page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains January 9 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>

        <h2 id="early">January 9 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Film Song, a Double A-Side and a Roller-Skate Novelty</h2>
        <p>The first three Januaries of the decade brought an Oscar winner, a double A-side and a roller-skate novelty.</p>

        <h3>1970: "Raindrops Keep Fallin' on My Head" by B. J. Thomas</h3>
        <p>Burt Bacharach and Hal David wrote the song for the 1969 film Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid.</p>
        <p>The two also produced B. J. Thomas's recording, which was released in October 1969.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it topped the Hot 100 for four weeks in January 1970.</p>
        <p>It won the Academy Award for Best Original Song.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data starts the run on January 3.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains January 9 was the first of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 4 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1971: "My Sweet Lord" by George Harrison</h3>
        <p>George Harrison wrote "My Sweet Lord" for his triple album All Things Must Pass.</p>
        <p>Harrison and Phil Spector co-produced it, and it was released in the US on November 23, 1970.</p>
        <p>In the US it was paired with "Isn't It a Pity" as a double A-side.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one on December 26 and stayed on top for four weeks.</p>
        <p>A court ruled in 1976 that Harrison had subconsciously copied the Chiffons' "He's So Fine," which Ronnie Mack wrote.</p>
        <p>The chart week that contains January 9 was the third of the four weeks.</p>
        <p>The song is number 31 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1972: "Brand New Key" by Melanie</h3>
        <p>Melanie wrote and sang "Brand New Key," and Peter Schekeryk produced it.</p>
        <p>It first appeared on her 1971 album Gather Me and came out as a single in October 1971.</p>
        <p>The song is sung from the viewpoint of a girl on roller skates trying to attract a boy's attention.</p>
        <p>Its chorus gave it the nickname "The Rollerskate Song," and Wikipedia says some places banned it from the radio.</p>
        <p>It also reached number one in Canada and Australia, and number four in the UK.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one, starting December 25, 1971.</p>
        <p>January 8 was the third and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 9 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">January 9 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Famous Mystery, a Posthumous Ballad and a Beatles Cover</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a song people still argue about.</p>
        <p>It also brought a ballad released after its writer's death and a cover with a famous guest.</p>

        <h3>1973: "You're So Vain" by Carly Simon</h3>
        <p>Carly Simon wrote "You're So Vain," and Richard Perry produced it.</p>
        <p>It was released as a single in November 1972 from the album No Secrets.</p>
        <p>Mick Jagger sang uncredited backing vocals.</p>
        <p>Simon has said the song refers to three men.</p>
        <p>The only one she has named publicly is the actor Warren Beatty.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it stayed at number one for the first three weeks of 1973.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data starts that run on January 6, so the January 9 week was the first.</p>
        <p>The song is number 9 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1974: "Time in a Bottle" by Jim Croce</h3>
        <p>Jim Croce wrote the lyrics after his wife, Ingrid, told him in December 1970 that she was pregnant.</p>
        <p>The song first appeared on his 1972 album You Don't Mess Around with Jim.</p>
        <p>Croce was killed in a plane crash in September 1973, and the single came out in November 1973.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it finished a two-week run at the top in early January 1974.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows the two weeks as December 29, 1973 and January 5, 1974.</p>
        <p>So January 5 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 24 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of his records are on <a href="/blog/artists/jim-croce/">Jim Croce</a>.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds" by Elton John</h3>
        <p>Elton John's version of the Beatles song came out as a single on November 15, 1974.</p>
        <p>It was credited to the Elton John Band.</p>
        <p>It was recorded at Caribou Ranch, and Davey Johnstone is credited with electric guitar and sitar.</p>
        <p>John Lennon sang backing vocals and played guitars, under the pseudonym Dr. Winston O'Boogie.</p>
        <p>It topped the Hot 100 for two weeks in January 1975.</p>
        <p>The RIAA certified it Gold on January 29, 1975.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data starts the run on January 4.</p>
        <p>So that chart week was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 35 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of his records are on <a href="/blog/artists/elton-john/">Elton John</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="late">January 9 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Two One-Week Hits and Two Bee Gees Records</h2>
        <p>The last four Januaries brought a spelled-out chant, a married duo's Grammy winner and two Bee Gees records.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Saturday Night" by the Bay City Rollers</h3>
        <p>Bill Martin and Phil Coulter wrote and produced "Saturday Night."</p>
        <p>The UK release came on June 29, 1973 and did not chart.</p>
        <p>The US release followed in August 1975.</p>
        <p>The song comes from the group's debut album Rollin', with Les McKeown on lead vocals.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia describes the word Saturday being spelled out in a rhythmic chant.</p>
        <p>It reached number one for one week and spent 17 weeks on the chart in total.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data puts that one week on the chart week of January 3, 1976.</p>
        <p>The song is number 64 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Don't Have to Be a Star (To Be in My Show)" by Marilyn McCoo and Billy Davis Jr.</h3>
        <p>James Dean and John Glover, both former Motown songwriters, wrote the song, and Don Davis produced it.</p>
        <p>It came out in September 1976, from the album I Hope We Get to Love in Time.</p>
        <p>Marilyn McCoo and Billy Davis Jr. were a husband and wife duo.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it hit number one on the Hot 100 and Hot Soul Singles charts.</p>
        <p>It won them the 1977 Grammy for Best R&amp;B Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocals.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of January 8, 1977.</p>
        <p>The song is number 27 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "How Deep Is Your Love" by the Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>Barry, Robin and Maurice Gibb wrote "How Deep Is Your Love."</p>
        <p>The demo was made at Le Château d'Hérouville in France, with more recording at Criteria Studios in Miami.</p>
        <p>It was released as a single in September 1977 and later used on the Saturday Night Fever soundtrack.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it stayed in the Top 10 for 17 weeks.</p>
        <p>It won the Grammy for Best Pop Performance by a Group at the 20th Grammy Awards.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows three weeks at number one from December 24, 1977.</p>
        <p>January 7 was the third and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 6 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/how-deep-is-your-love/">How Deep Is Your Love</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Too Much Heaven" by the Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>Barry, Robin and Maurice Gibb wrote "Too Much Heaven" as the group's contribution to the Music for UNICEF fund.</p>
        <p>They performed it at the Music for UNICEF Concert on January 9, 1979, which fell inside this very chart week.</p>
        <p>The single came out on October 24, 1978, as the first single from the album Spirits Having Flown.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it knocked "Le Freak" off the top spot for two weeks and was the fourth of six consecutive Bee Gees number ones.</p>
        <p>It earned over $7 million in publishing royalties.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data starts its two weeks on January 6, so the chart week that contains January 9 was the first.</p>
        <p>The song is number 11 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the group's records are on <a href="/blog/artists/bee-gees/">Bee Gees</a>.</p>

